(Abstract) Tj
T*
(While primary disease-control metrics are important, patient-reported functional status,) Tj
T*
(mental health, and treatment satisfaction define comprehensive IBD management success. We) Tj
T*
(report secondary analyses from a 52-week randomized trial of 98 IBD patients allocated to) Tj
T*
(intensive nursing support \(n=50\) or standard management \(n=48\). Secondary measures) Tj
T*
(evaluated sustained remission rates, durability of treatment response, patient) Tj
T*
(satisfaction with care coordination, functional limitations in work and daily activities,) Tj
T*
(and psychological well-being scores. By 52-week follow-up, a substantially higher) Tj
T*
(proportion of intensive-contact participants achieved and sustained disease remission.) Tj
T*
(Patients receiving intensive nursing support reported greater satisfaction with their care) Tj
T*
(experience and reported fewer activity limitations affecting employment and leisure.) Tj
T*
(Subgroup analyses suggested consistent benefits across disease phenotypes and baseline) Tj
T*
(disease severity. Qualitative exit interviews highlighted structured accountability and) Tj
T*
(regular provider contact as key satisfaction drivers. These secondary findings extend) Tj
T*
(primary-outcome data, demonstrating that systematic nursing engagement produces benefits) Tj
T*
(beyond immediate disease control to encompass functional and psychological dimensions of) Tj
